<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="apportionment-chapter">
  <title>Apportionment: Geometry, Fairness, and Representation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Every few years, the United States must decide how to distribute the fixed
      number of seats in the House of Representatives among the states.
      The same mathematical challenge appears in many governance decisions:
      allocating funding, assigning committee memberships, and distributing
      indivisible goods.
    </p>

    <p>
      In this chapter, we study how exact proportional shares can be represented
      as points inside a geometric space and how different rounding rules lead to
      distinct notions of fairness.
      This makes it easier to compare apportionment methods, identify paradoxes,
      and understand why no single rule can satisfy every reasonable criterion.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="apportionment-motivation">
    <title>Why Apportionment Matters</title>

    <p>
      Apportionment is not only an arithmetic problem.
      It is a governance decision because the chosen method affects which
      communities gain influence and which lose it.
    </p>

    <p>
      A method that seems reasonable in one context may appear unfair in another.
      For example, the United States uses apportionment to assign seats in the
      House of Representatives, while political parties use similar calculations
      to turn vote totals into council seats.
    </p>

    <p>
      Throughout this chapter, we will pay attention to the policy implications of
      the mathematics. When you compare methods, always note the assumptions about
      total seats, minimum representation, and the fairness criteria that matter
      in the governance setting.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="coffee-example">
    <title>Coffee for the Department</title>

    <p>
      To make the ideas concrete, we begin with a smaller allocation problem.
      Suppose the mathematics department has purchased <m>45</m> boxes of coffee
      and wants to distribute them to ten faculty members in proportion to the
      number of students each professor teaches.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="coffee-box-example">
      <title>Boxes of Coffee</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          The department decides that each professor's allocation should depend on
          the number of students they teach, including the professor themselves.
          Because boxes of coffee are indivisible, each professor must receive a
          whole number of boxes.
        </p>

        <table>
          <title>Faculty Teaching Loads</title>
          <tabular>
            <row header="yes">
              <cell>Professor</cell>
              <cell>Total Students</cell>
            </row>
            <row><cell>Ahmed</cell><cell>35</cell></row>
            <row><cell>Bowman</cell><cell>60</cell></row>
            <row><cell>Ceniceros</cell><cell>71</cell></row>
            <row><cell>Cockburn</cell><cell>22</cell></row>
            <row><cell>Dykstra</cell><cell>38</cell></row>
            <row><cell>Gibbons</cell><cell>55</cell></row>
            <row><cell>Kantrowitz</cell><cell>73</cell></row>
            <row><cell>Kuruwita</cell><cell>45</cell></row>
            <row><cell>LeMasurier</cell><cell>28</cell></row>
            <row><cell>Tripp</cell><cell>58</cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell><em>Total</em></cell>
              <cell><m>485</m></cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          The department will convert these counts into a proportional allocation
          of the <m>45</m> boxes.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <discussion xml:id="coffee-first-thoughts">
      <title>First Thoughts</title>

      <p>
        One way to start is to compute an ideal share for each professor.
        If the department treats the number of students as the relevant size,
        then the quota for professor <m>i</m> is
      </p>

      <me>
        q_i = \frac{a_i}{485} \cdot 45.
      </me>

      <p>
        For example, Ahmed's quota is
      </p>

      <me>
        q_{\text{Ahmed}} \approx 3.25,
        \qquad
        q_{\text{Kantrowitz}} \approx 6.77.
      </me>

      <p>
        These quotas are fractional, but the final allocation must be whole numbers.
      </p>
    </discussion>

    <discussion xml:id="coffee-rounding-problem">
      <title>The Rounding Problem</title>

      <p>
        If we round each quota to the nearest whole number, the result may not sum
        to the available number of boxes.
      </p>

      <p>
        For the coffee example, the quotas add up exactly to <m>45</m>, but ordinary
        rounding can produce too many or too few boxes.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          The difficulty of apportionment is that local rounding decisions need not
          preserve the global total.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
    </discussion>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="formal-definitions">
    <title>Formal Definitions</title>

    <definition xml:id="apportionment-definition">
      <title>Apportionment Problem</title>

      <p>
        An <term>apportionment problem</term> consists of a positive integer
        <m>M</m> representing the total number of indivisible items and a vector
        <m>\mathbf{a} = (a_1,\dots,a_n)</m> representing the sizes of the entities.
      </p>

      <p>
        The goal is to choose an integer allocation vector
        <m>\mathbf{m} = (m_1,\dots,m_n)</m> satisfying
      </p>

      <me>
        \sum_{j=1}^n m_j = M,
        \qquad
        m_j \in \mathbb Z_{\ge0} \text{ for all } j.
      </me>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="exact-quota-vector-definition">
      <title>Exact Quota Vector</title>

      <p>
        The <term>exact quota vector</term> is the ideal real allocation computed as
      </p>

      <me>
        q_j = \frac{a_j}{A} M,
        \qquad A = \sum_{i=1}^n a_i.
      </me>

      <p>
        The exact quota vector satisfies
      </p>

      <me>
        \sum_{j=1}^n q_j = M,
        \qquad q_j \ge 0.
      </me>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="quota-simplex-definition">
      <title>Quota Simplex</title>

      <p>
        The set of all exact quota vectors is
      </p>

      <me>
        \mathcal Q_M = \left\{(q_1,\dots,q_n) \in \mathbb R_{\ge0}^n :
        \sum_{j=1}^n q_j = M\right\}.
      </me>

      <p>
        This set is called the <term>quota simplex</term>.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="allocation-space-definition">
      <title>Allocation Space</title>

      <p>
        The set of feasible integer allocations is
      </p>

      <me>
        \mathcal M_M = \left\{(m_1,\dots,m_n) \in \mathbb Z_{\ge0}^n :
        \sum_{j=1}^n m_j = M\right\}.
      </me>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="apportionment-function-definition">
      <title>Apportionment Function</title>

      <p>
        An <term>apportionment function</term> is a rule
      </p>

      <me>
        F : \mathcal Q_M \to \mathcal M_M.
      </me>

      <p>
        It assigns each exact quota vector a unique integer allocation.
      </p>
    </definition>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="hamilton-method">
    <title>Hamilton's Method</title>

    <definition xml:id="hamilton-definition">
      <title>Hamilton's Method</title>

      <p>
        Hamilton's method begins by giving each entity its lower quota and then
        distributes remaining seats according to the largest fractional remainders.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>Compute the exact quota vector <m>\mathbf{q}</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Assign each entity <m>m_j = \lfloor q_j \rfloor</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Distribute remaining seats to the entities with the largest values of
          <m>q_j - \lfloor q_j \rfloor</m>.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      This defines a map <m>F_{\text{Ham}} : \mathcal Q_M \to \mathcal M_M</m>.
      Hamilton's method satisfies the quota rule but can violate house
      monotonicity.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="hamilton-coffee-example">
      <title>Applying Hamilton to Coffee</title>

      <p>
        For the coffee example, the exact quota vector is approximately
      </p>

      <me>
        \mathbf{q} \approx (3.25, 5.57, 6.59, 2.04, 3.53, 5.10, 6.77, 4.17,
        2.60, 5.38).
      </me>

      <p>
        The corresponding lower quotas are
      </p>

      <me>
        (3, 5, 6, 2, 3, 5, 6, 4, 2, 5).
      </me>

      <p>
        These sum to <m>41</m>, leaving <m>4</m> boxes to distribute by remainder.
      </p>

      <p>
        The four largest remainders correspond to Ceniceros, Kantrowitz, Bowman, and
        Tripp, so each receives one additional box under Hamilton's method.
      </p>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="divisor-methods">
    <title>Divisor Methods</title>

    <p>
      Divisor methods choose a single divisor and then round the ratios of entity
      sizes to that divisor. These methods are commonly used because they often
      satisfy monotonicity properties that Hamilton's method does not.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="divisor-method-definition">
      <title>Divisor Method</title>

      <p>
        Given a positive divisor <m>d</m>, compute
      </p>

      <me>
        x_j(d) = \frac{a_j}{d}.
      </me>

      <p>
        Choose <m>d</m> so that rounding each <m>x_j(d)</m> by a fixed rule yields
        allocations that sum to <m>M</m>.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <table>
      <title>Common Divisor Methods</title>
      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Method</cell>
          <cell>Rounding Rule</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Jefferson</cell>
          <cell>Always round down: <m>\lfloor x_j(d) \rfloor</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Adams</cell>
          <cell>Always round up: <m>\lceil x_j(d) \rceil</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Webster</cell>
          <cell>Round to the nearest integer</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Huntington–Hill</cell>
          <cell>Round using geometric mean thresholds</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Different divisor methods correspond to different partitions of the quota
      simplex. The choice of rounding rule determines which integer allocations
      are assigned to which regions.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="simplex-geometry">
    <title>Simplex Geometry and Barycentric Coordinates</title>

    <p>
      The set of all exact quota vectors is a geometric object called a simplex.
      For three entities, it is a triangle; for four, a tetrahedron; and for
      <m>n</m> entities, it is an <m>(n-1)</m>-dimensional simplex.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="affine-combination-definition">
      <title>Affine Combination</title>

      <p>
        A vector of the form
      </p>

      <me>
        \lambda_1 v_1 + \cdots + \lambda_k v_k
      </me>

      <p>
        is an <term>affine combination</term> of <m>v_1,\dots,v_k</m> when
      </p>

      <me>
        \sum_{i=1}^k \lambda_i = 1.
      </me>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="simplex-definition">
      <title>Simplex</title>

      <p>
        If <m>v_0,\dots,v_n</m> are affinely independent points in <m>\mathbb R^m</m>,
        the <term>simplex</term> they determine is
      </p>

      <me>
        \left\{\sum_{i=0}^n \lambda_i v_i : \lambda_i \ge 0,
        \sum_{i=0}^n \lambda_i = 1\right\}.
      </me>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="barycentric-definition">
      <title>Barycentric Coordinates</title>

      <p>
        A point in a simplex can be expressed uniquely as an affine combination
        of the simplex vertices. The coefficients are its <term>barycentric
        coordinates</term>.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="barycentric-example">
      <title>Barycentric Coordinates in the Quota Simplex</title>

      <p>
        In the quota simplex for three entities and <m>M=5</m>, the point
      </p>

      <me>
        (2,2,1)
      </me>

      <p>
        lies inside the simplex because its coordinates are nonnegative and sum to
        <m>5</m>. Relative to the vertices <m>(5,0,0), (0,5,0), (0,0,5)</m>, it has
        barycentric coordinates <m>(2/5, 2/5, 1/5)</m>.
      </p>
    </example>

    <discussion xml:id="simplex-interior-boundary">
      <title>Interior and Boundary Points</title>

      <p>
        A point is in the interior of the simplex when all barycentric coordinates
        are strictly positive. If any coordinate is zero, the point lies on a face
        of the simplex.
      </p>

      <p>
        For example, <m>(3,2,0)</m> lies on the edge between the first two
        vertices and corresponds to a quota that gives no seats to the third entity.
      </p>
    </discussion>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="apportionment-polyhedra">
    <title>Apportionment Polyhedra</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Any apportionment function partitions the quota simplex into regions such
        that all quotas in a given region map to the same integer allocation.
        These regions are called <term>apportionment polyhedra</term>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The boundaries between polyhedra correspond to tie situations where a small
        change in the quotas can change the allocation. These boundaries are where
        paradoxes are most likely to arise.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsubsection xml:id="hamilton-polyhedron">
      <title>Hamilton's Polyhedron</title>

      <p>
        Hamilton's method defines regions by the integer parts of quotas and the
        ordering of their fractional remainders. In the quota simplex, these
        conditions carve the simplex into a collection of polyhedral slabs.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="divisor-polyhedra">
      <title>Divisor Method Polyhedra</title>

      <p>
        Divisor methods define regions using thresholds that depend on the rounding
        rule. The choice of divisor and rounding preference changes the partition
        of the simplex, and thus the possible integer allocations.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="fairness-criteria">
    <title>Fairness Criteria and Paradoxes</title>

    <definition xml:id="quota-rule-definition">
      <title>Quota Rule</title>

      <p>
        A method satisfies the <term>quota rule</term> if each allocation <m>m_j</m>
        is either <m>\lfloor q_j \rfloor</m> or <m>\lceil q_j \rceil</m>.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="house-monotonicity-definition">
      <title>House Monotonicity</title>

      <p>
        A method is <term>house monotone</term> if increasing the total number of
        seats cannot decrease any entity's allocation.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="population-monotonicity-definition">
      <title>Population Monotonicity</title>

      <p>
        A method is <term>population monotone</term> if a growing entity never
        loses a seat to an entity whose size does not grow.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="alabama-paradox-definition">
      <title>Alabama Paradox</title>

      <p>
        The <term>Alabama Paradox</term> occurs when increasing the total number
        of seats causes some entity to lose a seat.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="population-paradox-definition">
      <title>Population Paradox</title>

      <p>
        The <term>population paradox</term> occurs when one entity gains population
        faster than another but still loses a seat while the other gains one.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="new-states-paradox-definition">
      <title>New States Paradox</title>

      <p>
        The <term>new states paradox</term> occurs when adding a new entity changes
        allocations among existing entities.
      </p>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Hamilton's method satisfies the quota rule but can violate house
      monotonicity. Jefferson's method is house monotone but can violate the
      quota rule. These tradeoffs show why different methods prioritize different
      governance fairness principles.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="balinski-young">
    <title>Balinski–Young Impossibility Theorem</title>

    <theorem xml:id="balinski-young-theorem">
      <title>Balinski–Young</title>

      <p>
        No apportionment method can satisfy both the quota rule and house
        monotonicity for every possible apportionment problem.
      </p>
    </theorem>

    <discussion>
      <title>Interpreting the Theorem</title>

      <p>
        The Balinski–Young Theorem formalizes an unavoidable tradeoff: a method can
        be good at preserving quotas or good at avoiding paradoxes when the total
        number of seats changes, but it cannot do both perfectly in all cases.
      </p>

      <p>
        From a governance perspective, this theorem says that choosing an
        apportionment method is a choice between competing fairness objectives.
      </p>
    </discussion>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="us-house-case-study">
    <title>Case Study: U.S. House Apportionment</title>

    <p>
      In U.S. congressional apportionment, the total number of seats is fixed at
      <m>435</m>, and each state must receive at least one seat.
      Exact quotas are computed from the decennial census population counts.
    </p>

    <p>
      Current law specifies the Huntington–Hill method, a divisor method whose
      rounding threshold is based on the geometric mean of successive integers.
      This method was chosen to balance fairness principles and avoid the most
      obvious paradoxes.
    </p>

    <p>
      The quota simplex for the United States is high-dimensional, and the
      apportionment function partitions that simplex into regions corresponding to
      different allocations for the states.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="apportionment-exercises">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="coffee-quota-calculation">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the coffee example, compute the exact quota vector
          <m>\mathbf{q}</m>.
        </p>

        <ol marker="a">
          <li>
            <p>Verify that the quotas sum to <m>45</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Compute the lower quota vector <m>\lfloor \mathbf{q} \rfloor</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>How many boxes remain after assigning the lower quotas?</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="hamilton-allocation">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Apply Hamilton's method to the coffee quotas from the example.
        </p>

        <ol marker="a">
          <li>
            <p>List the four largest fractional remainders.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Give the final allocation of the <m>45</m> boxes.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Does the allocation satisfy the quota rule? Explain.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="simplex-membership">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the simplex
        </p>

        <me>
          \{(q_1,q_2,q_3) \in \mathbb R_{\ge0}^3 : q_1+q_2+q_3 = 10\}.
        </me>

        <ol marker="a">
          <li>
            <p>Does the point <m>(3,4,3)</m> lie in this simplex?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Find the barycentric coordinates of the point
              <m>(7,2,1)</m> with respect to the standard vertices.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Describe the face of the simplex where <m>q_3 = 0</m>.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="balinski-young-explanation">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why the Balinski–Young Theorem shows that no single
          apportionment method can satisfy both the quota rule and house
          monotonicity for every possible problem.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="method-comparison">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compare Jefferson's method and Hamilton's method on the coffee example.
        </p>

        <ol marker="a">
          <li>
            <p>Which method satisfies the quota rule?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Which method is house monotone?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Which method is more likely to favor larger entities? Why?</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
